**Code Explanation:**

The code loads the unwrapped trajectories and time array generated in Step 1. It computes the ensemble-averaged Mean-Square Displacement (MSD) and its directional components (MSD_x, MSD_y, MSD_z) by calculating the squared displacements of all tracers relative to their initial positions at t=0. The Root-Mean-Square Displacement (RMSD) is also computed. To quantify the anisotropy of the dispersion, the ratio lambda(t) = max(MSD_x, MSD_y, MSD_z) / min(MSD_x, MSD_y, MSD_z) is calculated at each time step, and its time-averaged value is printed. The global power-law exponent alpha is estimated by performing a linear regression on the log-log transformed MSD vs. time lag data (excluding t=0). To analyze the time-dependent scaling behavior, the local exponent alpha(t) is computed as the derivative of log(MSD) with respect to log(t) using finite differences. A Savitzky-Golay filter is applied to smooth the derivative and reduce numerical noise. Based on the smoothed alpha(t), the code identifies and prints the time ranges corresponding to different diffusion regimes: Ballistic (alpha >= 1.8), Superdiffusive (1.2 < alpha < 1.8), Diffusive (0.8 <= alpha <= 1.2), and Subdiffusive (alpha < 0.8). Finally, all computed arrays are saved to the data/ directory for downstream analysis and visualization.

